In our network packet parser (file: src/parser.c), the function `parse_hex_string` converts a hex string from a network buffer into a byte array. The function signature is:

```c
int parse_hex_string(const char *hex, size_t hex_len, unsigned char *out, size_t out_cap);
```

It is supposed to parse exactly `hex_len` characters (each pair represents one byte) and write the resulting bytes into `out` (which has capacity `out_cap`). The function currently has a bug that causes a buffer overrun when `hex_len` is odd. For example, calling `parse_hex_string("abc", 3, buf, 2)` writes 2 bytes into `buf` (which has capacity 2) but actually writes 3 bytes, corrupting adjacent memory. Also, when `hex_len` is even and `out_cap` is exactly half of `hex_len`, it works fine, but the code might be off-by-one in the loop condition.

We need you to fix the bug. The function should:
- Return -1 if the input length is odd or if `out_cap` is less than `hex_len/2`.
- Parse each pair of hex digits into a byte, using a helper `hex_val` that converts a single hex digit to its value (0-15) or -1 if invalid.
- Write the bytes into `out`.
- Return the number of bytes written on success.

Please inspect the current implementation and provide a corrected version. Also, explain the exact cause of the off-by-one error.

Current code (from src/parser.c):

```c
#include <stddef.h>

static int hex_val(char c) {
    if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') return c - '0';
    if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') return c - 'a' + 10;
    if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') return c - 'A' + 10;
    return -1;
}

int parse_hex_string(const char *hex, size_t hex_len, unsigned char *out, size_t out_cap) {
    if (hex == NULL || out == NULL || hex_len % 2 != 0) return -1;
    size_t bytes_needed = hex_len / 2;
    if (bytes_needed > out_cap) return -1;
    size_t i, j = 0;
    for (i = 0; i < hex_len; i += 2) {
        int hi = hex_val(hex[i]);
        int lo = hex_val(hex[i+1]);
        if (hi == -1 || lo == -1) return -1;
        out[j++] = (unsigned char)((hi << 4) | lo);
    }
    return j;
}
```

